@prefix ds: <https://ds.canonical.com/>.
@prefix anatomy: <https://anatomy.canonical.com/>.
@prefix dt: <https://dt.canonical.com/>.

ds:concept.Design-tokens-Overview a ds:Concept;
    ds:name "Design tokens: Overview";
    ds:summary "This document outlines how we set up, manage, structure and update our Design tokens. The actual values of the tokens are defined separately, in:\n\n- Typography tokens\n- Color tokens\n- Dimension tokens";
    ds:tier ds:global;
    ds:content "# **Overview**\n\nDesign tokens are reusable, stable constants that store the smallest visual properties in our Design system, such as color, typography, and spacing. They facilitate consistency across different platforms and technologies, making it easier to update and scale the visual appearance of a Design system.\n\nThe w3c [Design Tokens Community Group](https://www.designtokens.org/) announced in October 2025 the first stable version of the [Design Tokens Specification](https://www.designtokens.org/technical-reports/). This specification provides the first industry standard, production-ready, vendor-neutral format for sharing design decisions across tools and platforms - and the Pragma design system is basing its tokens on this spec. \n\nStoring Design tokens centrally in a platform-agnostic way (as JSON in a git repository) makes it easier to implement the same Design system on different platforms, from CSS to Flutter or Figma. The mature processes of git for contributions are a perfect fit for maintaining a stable set of tokens.\n\nFor the Canonical theme of the PRagma design system, design tokens are stored in github, at [https://github.com/canonical/pragma-core/tree/main/packages/tokens](https://github.com/canonical/pragma-core/tree/main/packages/tokens)\n\nPrimitive Design tokens reduce the decision space by restricting possible choices to available primitive tokens. For example, when deciding on which color to use for a new component, only colors that exist as primitive tokens are available. This restriction alone is a major advantage of Design tokens, helping to scale visual designers' decisions to a larger team and making the Design system more consistent. A full semantic token system makes it easy to apply the right token for the right use case.\n\n\n\n# **Design token taxonomy**\n\nAs Design tokens are intended to be used across different platforms, they must be defined in a platform-agnostic way. In order to be able to re-implement a Design system on different platforms, each visual design decision must be tokenized. This means that a considerable number of tokens must be created.\n\nTherefore, it is important to create a well-crafted Design token taxonomy that can be used on any platform and is scalable for 10s, 100s, or 1000s of tokens.\n\n## **Token categories**\n\nAt the highest level, tokens are categorized into one of the following three categories:\n\n- **Primitive tokens**\n  - Primitive tokens represent the visual design foundation of the Design system by encapsulating the raw values, restricting the possible decision space. This means that when a new visual design decision has to be made, for example, not every possible color is available, but only those that exist as primitive tokens. \n  - This restriction alone represents a major advantage of Design tokens and thus scales the decisions of visual designers well to a larger team, making the Design system more consistent.\n    - eg. `blue-500` *= #24598f*\n- **Semantic tokens**. While the primitive tokens represent the possible visual decisions that can be made, semantic tokens are supposed to make it easier to apply these primitive tokens to the right use case. They do this by having semantic token names, i.e. describing their purpose or function within the Design system. The value of a semantic token is always supposed to be a primitive token or another semantic token, never a raw value.\n    - eg. `color-background-default` *= blue-500*\n- **Component tokens**. Component tokens represent the most specific level in the token hierarchy. They define the visual properties of individual UI elements, such as buttons, cards, or navigation bars. These tokens apply semantic and primitive tokens to create cohesive, reusable components. \n    - eg. color-background-button-primary-default *= color-background-default*\n\nThe 3 types increase in order of specificity, but decrease in versatility of use.\n\nThere is a tension between specificity and scope. If you are not specific enough in certain areas, people will end up choosing the wrong tokens. If you create too many specific tokens, you end up with a lot of tokens with very narrow use cases that will overwhelm and confuse people. \n\nOne way to address this problem is to only go down to component tokens in some cases, such as overwriting default values or for the most-used components. For instance, a bordered code snippet (1px) does not use the default (1.5px). In other cases, components can just use semantic tokens instead, which would still cover theming and responsiveness out of the box. \n\nHowever, if using a semantic token seems too ambiguous and people might be confused about which token to use for a component, then it's better to create a specific component token (and reference the appropriate semantic or primitive token).\n\nUsing **primitive tokens directly** in components or designs **is strongly discouraged** as it makes it harder for new designers and developers to understand how and why a token was applied.\n\n## **Token name parts**\n\nToken name parts are, as the name suggests, the different parts that make up a token name. Those are often called Design token levels or dynamics. There are different approaches/frameworks that are used. For our Design tokens we agreed to use CTI - Category, Type, Item.\n\nIn CTI there are the following potential name parts:\n\n- Domain (optional)\n  - An optional 2–4 letter prefix that identifies the system the token belongs to. This name part is only required if there are multiple systems that have tokens and we want to make it easy to differentiate to which system this token belongs to.\n- **Category**\n  - What the token is. (eg. color, dimension, opacity)\n- Mode (optional)\n  - If the same token exists in different modes (eg. light, dark etc.) this name part indicates for which of those modes this token is intended for. This is only used if there is a mode for the category.\n- **Type**\n  - How the token is used. (eg. background, text, icon, border)\n- Item\n  - The component it’s used on. (eg. Button, Modal, Card)\n- Variant\n  - Variants of the component (eg. default, primary, secondary, warning)\n- State\n  - For example interactive states (eg. default, hover, active etc.) or sizes (eg. small, medium, large)\n- Context\n  - Color behavior relative to a background or the intrinsic size of the item. (onDark, onLight, 8ColumnsWide)\n\n\n\nAll tokens have to have at least a category name part plus a type, item or variant name part. A token name can have more than one state and more than one variant.\n\nIt is also possible to repeat name parts. For example if you need to further define where in a component a token is applied you can repeat the item name part for example: color-background-slider-track which would be category-type-item-item. This name part repeating pattern can also be used to create multidimensional themes: color-background-canonical-light-default which would be category-type-mode-mode-variant.\n\n## **Token name part order**\n\nCTI can be ordered in different ways: CTI and ITC (Item, Type, Category). The order impacts both the token's final name and its definition's nesting structure. For instance, with CTI, all colors are grouped together. Conversely, using ITC (Item, Type, Category) results in all tokens for a specific component being grouped.\n\n​\n\nWe have agreed to use the CTI order. It makes more sense to group the tokens by category than by element/component. For example, it makes more sense to compare the background color of a button to the background of the page it is on rather than the text size of the button's label.\n\n# **Token value standardization**\n\nFor all of the categories we want to stay consistent in how we save the value of a token. The way in which the values of a category are saved are to be defined in the specification of the tokens of a category.\n\n## **Token scales**\n\nThere are two different naming schemes we have for scales in design tokens: a scale based on **magic numbers** and a naming scheme based on **t-shirt** sizes.\n\nWe think that it is necessary to have at least two scale naming schemes, as we will have scales both in primitive tokens and semantic tokens. Using the same scale naming scheme for both would be confusing, as they would not necessarily be related to each other.\n\nIn the following paragraph we are going to explain what these scales look like, the advantages of each, and in which use cases they should be used.\n\n### Magic numbers\n\nA magic number scale usually looks something like this: 100, 200, 300, 400 etc. It can be based on 10s, 100s, 1000s or any other base. The base is defined by its use cases and the personal choice of the person creating the scale\n\nOne of the main advantages of a magic number based scale is that there is no fixed number of tokens in the scale. For example if your initial token set is 100, 200, 300, 400, 500 and at a later point you realise that you desperately need a value between 200 and 300 you can introduce a 250 token without having to rename any other tokens to accommodate this new token.\n\nAnother advantage is that it makes it easy to make calculations based on the magic number of the token. You can create a function which takes a magic number step as an input and from that derives the value of that step. \n\nFor example, our dimension tokens could be:\n\n`dimension-100` is 0.5rem and `dimension-200` is 1rem. So, in this case, it's as simple as dividing the magic number step by 200 to get the rem value of the dimension token. And this relationship between magic number steps is quite easy to understand and, therefore, an advantage of the magic number scale.\n\nThese functions can become more complex, as will be the case for our color palette. We will calculate the color based on the magic number step, which makes generating the palette easier. What exactly the calculation is will not be as obvious as for the dimensions.\n\nThis relation between tokens is quite easy to understand with magic number-based tokens, making magic number-based scales usually quite intuitive.\n\n### **T-shirt sizes**\n\nT-shirt size based scales usually look like this: xxs, xs, s, m, l, xl, xxl.\n\nBoth an advantage and disadvantage of t-shirt sizes is that it's a pretty fixed scale. Theoretically you can extend the edges by adding more x’s, but the middle of the scale is fixed. For example, no new values can be introduced between M and L. \n\nThis means that t-shirt size scales are best used for a scale in which the number of values should be restricted.  For example, usually you don’t want to allow infinitely many radii in a design system but keep it at most to something like 5. And usually there is also no pressing need to introduce a new radius between radius-m and radius-l.\n\nWhile magic number based scales almost necessitate a defined relationship between each value, that is not necessarily the case for t-shirt sizes. There is more leeway to not have a hard computational relationship between for example s and xxl.\n\nWhile the relation between tokens is therefore maybe not as clear as with magic numbers, t-shirt sizes are slightly more semantic. It is easier to understand what to apply a “large” token to compared to a “800” token.\n\n### **When to use each?**\n\n**Magic numbers** should only be used for primitive tokens to define the base scale of that category. So for example in the color category for the base color palette or in dimensions for the base dimensions scale. Here the advantage of being able to add new tokens after the fact is important, as the needs for new base values in a category might come up as the design system scales.\n\n**T-shirt** sizes *can* be used for primitive tokens, but only if it is necessary to differentiate from an already existing magic number scale in the same category, and if few values are expected (generally 5 or less). For example in the dimension category we would have a set of base dimension values based on a magic number scale. But radii will not be based on this scale so we also need to introduce a primitive scale for radii. This scale can and should be based on t-shirt sizes to differentiate it from the main primitive dimension scale and to already introduce a constraint on the amount of primitive radii token that can be created.\n\n**T-shirt** sizes *must* be used in semantic tokens. For example to create a font scale based on the primitive dimension tokens.\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n# References: \n\n[Canonical Internal]: [Spec DE015 - Design Tokens](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1G45-Q3hUDhD6X7B6tSZiiME4euANxsyVdfS4pt9899w/edit?usp=sharing)\n\n[External]: [Design Tokens Community Group](https://www.designtokens.org/)";
    ds:knownEdgeCases "";
    ds:conceptType ds:concepttype.Explanation.
